Deep work — the ability to focus without distraction for extended periods — is the primary driver of high-value knowledge work (Cal Newport, Deep Work, 2016); remote environments structurally enable more of it.
The same Microsoft study found the number of meetings decreased 18% for remote workers, reducing coordination overhead and returning time to productive work.
Neuroscience shows that context-switching (moving between tasks due to interruptions) consumes prefrontal glucose and degrades decision quality cumulatively — fewer interruptions directly improves cognitive output.
GitLab, Basecamp, and Zapier — all fully remote organisations — consistently ship software with small teams at a rate that outpaces much larger co-located competitors.
Acoustic analysis of open-plan offices shows average noise levels of 60–65 dB — equivalent to a restaurant — which cognitive science identifies as the threshold above which reading comprehension begins to degrade.
